A||||4||AkursedX||Another Tranny Question||||5||
Z||000000||AkursedX||09-25-2000||10:33 PM||||Ok, well I posted a topic on here a little while ago about my torque converter lockup selonoid going on my car.  Well I unplugged the connector and my problems have disappeared.<P>My question is, does unplugging the seloniod have any detrimental effects to my transmission?  Winter is coming around, so I'll only drive my car for about another month, and then i'll fix it over the winter, (throwing in a shift kit right away), but I just want to make sure that I'm not hurting anything in there seriousley. <BR>I know my fair share about engines, but when it comes to tranny's, I know nothing.<P>Any feedback is greatly appreciated.  Thanks in advance  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||09-25-2000||11:53 PM||||I'm pretty sure you're okay.  The torque converter clutch is there to help fuel mileage, mainly.<P>If you realy worked at it, you could talk yourself into thinking that running w/o TCC makes the torque converter generate more heat.  And it does.  Significantly more heat?  Probably not.  Enough to hurt the transmission?  I doubt that very much.<P>Remember, for many years automatics didn't even have TCC's.   I had a THM in an Olds Cutlass, think the tranny fluid was changed once, and it outlived the car.    ||||reg||14
Z||000002||AkursedX||09-26-2000||12:11 AM||||Thanks alot.  I didn't think there would be any problems, but i just wanted some more expertise info.  I did notice a little bit of a mileage drop though, but i can live with that for now.  Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000003||theogre||09-26-2000||08:48 AM||||This part is available thru the GM dealer network and other sources. You can change it yourself with common tools. The switch is under the side cover of the tranny. you can get to it thru the wheel well w/o messing with the cradle. ((Altho if you have an 86+ with the single tranny mount you will need to fool with supporting the tranny to remove the mount bracket.))<P>GM's official stance looks to be that while the transmission may not up and die over night the increased heat will shorten it's life. Cooling capacity for the TH125 is calculated for running the TC locked most of the time.<P>With the price of gas, I'd fix anything that makes a noticable drop in millage ASAP. I've lost 5 mpg and I think it's at least part due to a cracked exhaust manifiold. The manifold is getting replaced as soon as I can afford one. (Some of it is likely due to the way I get to work now. Can't help that tho. there's no highway route there that isn't under construction.)||||reg||1
Z||000004||dman||09-26-2000||03:44 PM||||Hello everyone. I'm in the process of getting my convertible up and running. The speedo wasn't working and I fixed that over the weekend. I took it for a test drive and when I got off the freeway the car died. I knew right away that the TCC solenoid was locked up. Now that the ECM was getting a valid VSS signal, it was deciding to lock up the solenoid (which it was never able to do with the speedo not working).<P>None of the regular parts store (on Sunday) had the part. Yesterday I ended up finding a trannie parts store a couple of miles away, the solenoid was $16 bucks and the side cover gasket was a couple bucks. I decided not to get the 3rd gear switch.<P>Anyways, here are couple of links for you in case you don't already know the procedure:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tcc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tcc.html</A> <P>Don't forget to check out ogre's cave for a good article on the TH125C transmission.||||reg||1
